<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lotf Ali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">executed <a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lotf Ali Khan was the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. A brave and capable soldier, he resisted the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader Agha Mohammad Khan for several years. He was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and executed in 1794, ending the Zand dynasty and opening the way for Qajar rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan.jpg" alt="Lotf Ali Khan" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nimazandyf · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lotfali_khan_zand2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lotf Ali Khan was born about 1769. He came to power after a decade of infighting among violent Zand chiefs following the death of the dynasty's founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>, in 1779. Their failure to agree on a successor eroded public faith in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a>, and more local and regional leaders aligned with the eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a>, who sought to succeed them. Lotf Ali was the son of Jafar Khan, who was poisoned by a slave bribed by the rival Sayed Morad Khan Zand. He claimed the throne in 1789, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and forced Sayed Morad Khan to surrender, and the rival was executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Agha Mohammad Khan marched south soon afterwards and defeated Lotf Ali outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> to frighten the Zand horses, but Lotf Ali held the city. A campaign against the Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 1790 failed in harsh winter weather. In 1791, while he was marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, Haji Ibrahim, the kalantar of Shiraz, seized the city, and most of Lotf Ali's men deserted. He raised a small force near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> and later defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> armies, then struck the camp of Agha Mohammad Khan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> at night. He withdrew to wait for sunrise, and the Qajar army regrouped, so he had to retreat.</p><p>He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 1792 and then to Tabas, and in July 1792 his family and other Zand nobles were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. In 1794 he took Kerman and held it for four months against Agha Mohammad Khan. Troops then opened the citadel gates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> army. The Qajar ruler took brutal revenge on the people of Kerman: male inhabitants were killed or blinded, women and children were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the city was destroyed over ninety days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lotf Ali was betrayed by the ruler of Bam and captured nearby. It is reported that he was blinded, imprisoned and tortured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> before being choked to death late in 1794. His defeat ended the 44-year Zand reign and began the 131-year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a>. By tradition he wrote a poem in his own blood on his cell wall.</p><p>Contemporary and later sources describe him as tall and handsome, skilled with the sword and brave, and the British writer Sir Harford Jones Brydges called him the last chivalrous figure among the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. His tomb is in Imamzadeh Zeyd in the Old Bazaar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, and a main avenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lotf Ali Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1794 he captured Kerman and the last Zand ruler, <b>Lotf Ali Khan</b>, who was blinded and killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1791 the governor of Shiraz, Haji Ebrahim Khan Kalantar, betrayed the last Zand ruler, <b>Lotf Ali Khan</b>, and handed the city to the Qajars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Malayer, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.29,48.82&amp;year=1794" aria-label="Malayer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5792 921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lotf Ali Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6242 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6242 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.29,48.82&amp;year=1794">What was here in 1794? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
